WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.970
SPEAKER 1: Now, if you're
going to have consent, that's

00:00:02.970 --> 00:00:05.880
a very radical thing to think.

00:00:05.880 --> 00:00:08.038
The King of England
didn't think that.

00:00:08.038 --> 00:00:09.472
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:16.170 --> 00:00:19.620
What is consent, and why?

00:00:19.620 --> 00:00:22.080
SPEAKER 2: It's kind of more
or less a social contract,

00:00:22.080 --> 00:00:25.440
that both parties are agreeing
to the terms and conditions.

00:00:25.440 --> 00:00:27.930
We as the people
being governed are

00:00:27.930 --> 00:00:31.770
allowing those who are in
power to look over and provide

00:00:31.770 --> 00:00:34.050
the necessary
security so we can,

00:00:34.050 --> 00:00:36.642
as they put, to
pursue our happiness.

00:00:36.642 --> 00:00:38.850
SPEAKER 1: We might stick
with just something direct,

00:00:38.850 --> 00:00:40.920
and simple, and understandable.

00:00:40.920 --> 00:00:44.130
Like, if we're
equal, you wouldn't

00:00:44.130 --> 00:00:45.990
have the authority
to do something

00:00:45.990 --> 00:00:49.530
to me unless I say so.

00:00:49.530 --> 00:00:53.190
Whereas the king answered the
Declaration of Independence--

00:00:53.190 --> 00:00:54.630
did you know that?

00:00:54.630 --> 00:00:58.780
He gave an address from the
throne, and he gave an answer.

00:00:58.780 --> 00:01:03.150
And he basically said in there,
I was born to be the king here,

00:01:03.150 --> 00:01:05.820
and you were born
to be the subjects.

00:01:05.820 --> 00:01:09.270
And I am required to look
after you, and be good to you,

00:01:09.270 --> 00:01:11.970
and take care of you, and to do
what's in your interest, what's

00:01:11.970 --> 00:01:13.180
good for you.

00:01:13.180 --> 00:01:15.540
But you are required
to do what I say.

00:01:15.540 --> 00:01:18.580
And he thought that would
turn the whole thing around.

00:01:18.580 --> 00:01:20.700
And at the moment that
he distributed this thing

00:01:20.700 --> 00:01:26.220
across the lines to
the Continental Army,

00:01:26.220 --> 00:01:30.750
it was melting away because
their enlistments were up.

00:01:30.750 --> 00:01:34.050
And they read that thing
and they went, what?

00:01:34.050 --> 00:01:35.880
Is that what he thinks?

00:01:35.880 --> 00:01:37.230
We're staying.

00:01:37.230 --> 00:01:39.300
We're going to do
this thing, right?

00:01:39.300 --> 00:01:43.200
The king's idea is that I
was born with an obligation,

00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:44.790
that you were born
with an obligation.

00:01:44.790 --> 00:01:49.800
We both have to do our thing,
and we're born that way.

00:01:49.800 --> 00:01:52.170
And the Declaration of
Independence denies that.

00:01:52.170 --> 00:01:55.920
It says, we're all born equal.

00:01:55.920 --> 00:01:58.420
So that's what they say.

00:01:58.420 --> 00:02:02.790
And if it's like
that, then you get

00:02:02.790 --> 00:02:06.872
to be our king if we say so.

00:02:06.872 --> 00:02:08.789
There's a really great
document that we're not

00:02:08.789 --> 00:02:10.949
reading in this course,
but I encourage everyone

00:02:10.949 --> 00:02:12.930
to read it in our
Constitution reader.

00:02:12.930 --> 00:02:15.810
It's written by Thomas
Jefferson, who, of course, was

00:02:15.810 --> 00:02:18.690
a very powerful writer--

00:02:18.690 --> 00:02:21.510
Earth-shakingly powerful
writer, wrote the Declaration

00:02:21.510 --> 00:02:22.568
of Independence.

00:02:22.568 --> 00:02:25.110
And it's called A Summary View
of the Rights of British North

00:02:25.110 --> 00:02:26.320
America.

00:02:26.320 --> 00:02:30.210
And in there, he goes on and
on and on, and he explains,

00:02:30.210 --> 00:02:33.330
you get to be the
king if we say so.

00:02:33.330 --> 00:02:34.770
You have to do what we say.

00:02:34.770 --> 00:02:37.320
In the last paragraph,
which I'll paraphrase,

00:02:37.320 --> 00:02:42.390
he says let those
flatter who fear.

00:02:42.390 --> 00:02:47.140
It is not an American art.

00:02:47.140 --> 00:02:54.250
Your counselors are partisans,
not drawn from us, right?

00:02:54.250 --> 00:02:58.420
So he's telling
the king what for.

00:02:58.420 --> 00:03:01.390
And it gives rise soon--

00:03:01.390 --> 00:03:07.480
the flag with the snake
that says Don't Tread On Me.

00:03:07.480 --> 00:03:08.980
We have a right.

00:03:08.980 --> 00:03:11.620
We are born the same as you.

00:03:11.620 --> 00:03:15.680
You get your carriage,
since you got it already.

00:03:15.680 --> 00:03:19.300
It's just that you don't get
to tell us what to do from it.

00:03:19.300 --> 00:03:23.440
Consent is born in equality
in that way, right?

00:03:23.440 --> 00:03:28.630
And you just have to
remember that we Americans,

00:03:28.630 --> 00:03:31.990
we just breathe that from birth.

00:03:31.990 --> 00:03:34.630
We get that with mother's milk.

00:03:34.630 --> 00:03:36.850
That's what we think.

00:03:36.850 --> 00:03:39.830
We think we're born equal.

00:03:39.830 --> 00:03:44.470
It's the reason why we
think we can do whatever

00:03:44.470 --> 00:03:46.990
we want if we work hard enough.

00:03:46.990 --> 00:03:49.510
It's the reason I thought--

00:03:49.510 --> 00:03:53.260
my dad's a schoolteacher
from Pocahontas, Arkansas--

00:03:53.260 --> 00:03:57.280
but I didn't know that wasn't
the grandest thing in the world

00:03:57.280 --> 00:03:58.540
to be.

00:03:58.540 --> 00:04:00.910
I knew we weren't
rich, but I didn't

00:04:00.910 --> 00:04:03.850
think anybody was born
better than I, nor I better

00:04:03.850 --> 00:04:05.650
than anybody else.

00:04:05.650 --> 00:04:10.120
Churchill used to love to
say about equality, he'd say,

00:04:10.120 --> 00:04:12.400
it's the principle
that everybody

00:04:12.400 --> 00:04:15.730
is as good as everybody
else, or a little better.

00:04:15.730 --> 00:04:17.579
[LAUGHTER]

00:04:17.579 --> 00:04:18.880
You like that?

00:04:18.880 --> 00:04:20.500
Churchill was very witty.

00:04:20.500 --> 00:04:24.380
So now, if you're
going to have consent,

00:04:24.380 --> 00:04:27.430
that's a very radical
thing to think.

00:04:27.430 --> 00:04:30.040
The King of England
didn't think that, right?

00:04:30.040 --> 00:04:33.070
The fact that he gave that
address from the throne

00:04:33.070 --> 00:04:35.470
and had it distributed--

00:04:35.470 --> 00:04:39.040
he thought that
would be persuasive.

00:04:39.040 --> 00:04:45.370
Later, they send Ben
Franklin, who was, let's say,

00:04:45.370 --> 00:04:47.362
a very artful human being.

00:04:47.362 --> 00:04:48.820
We all know what
that means, right?

00:04:48.820 --> 00:04:50.380
[LAUGHTER]

00:04:50.380 --> 00:04:53.230
For example, he was the
world's greatest spokesman

00:04:53.230 --> 00:04:57.790
for pious middle class morality.

00:04:57.790 --> 00:05:01.073
Anyway, they sent him
to England to negotiate.

00:05:01.073 --> 00:05:01.990
He didn't get on well.

00:05:01.990 --> 00:05:03.790
They liked him better in Paris.

00:05:03.790 --> 00:05:06.250
He went there, too.

00:05:06.250 --> 00:05:08.650
And he gets over there and
he talks with the cabinet.

00:05:08.650 --> 00:05:12.370
And the cabinet says, we
are the mother country,

00:05:12.370 --> 00:05:14.200
and you are the children.

00:05:14.200 --> 00:05:16.990
And you must do what we say.

00:05:16.990 --> 00:05:19.810
And he writes a letter back
to the Continental Congress

00:05:19.810 --> 00:05:22.030
and says, there's
going to be a war.

00:05:22.030 --> 00:05:23.800
[LAUGHTER]

00:05:23.800 --> 00:05:26.110
Because what they think--

00:05:26.110 --> 00:05:31.600
and remember, what they think
is what everybody thinks.

00:05:31.600 --> 00:05:33.820
It's what the King of
France thinks, who later

00:05:33.820 --> 00:05:35.088
subsidized our revolution.

00:05:35.088 --> 00:05:36.130
It's what they all think.

00:05:36.130 --> 00:05:40.000
It's what the world has
thought from time immemorial.

00:05:40.000 --> 00:05:41.950
And these guys don't think that.

00:05:41.950 --> 00:05:46.810
That's a remarkable
development in human history.

00:05:46.810 --> 00:05:48.260
SPEAKER 3: Thank
you for watching.

00:05:48.260 --> 00:05:50.230
We hope you're enjoying
our Highlights series

00:05:50.230 --> 00:05:53.140
and invite you to explore
all of Hillsdale College's

00:05:53.140 --> 00:05:54.310
online courses.

00:05:54.310 --> 00:05:58.470
They are free and for
everyone who loves to learn.

